# pixi
.pixi/
*.egg-info/
# python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
# Coverage artifacts. `test-cov` writes `.coverage`, but under xdist every worker first writes its
# own `.coverage.<host>.<pid>.<random>` and they are combined afterwards -- so the glob is not
# optional, and a run that dies mid-way leaves the per-worker files behind. Nothing here is anyone's
# deliverable: it is a line-hit database for one run on one machine, rewritten by the next.
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml
build/
dist/
# seqforge working state (resumable, content-addressed — never committed).
#
# ANCHORED, and it has to be. An unanchored `seqforge/` matches a directory of that name at ANY
# depth, which in this repo is `src/seqforge/` — git would ignore our own source tree, silently.
# The leading slash pins it to the repo root, which is where a `-C .` run puts it.
/seqforge/
.seqforge/
# docs build
site/
# editor / OS
.DS_Store
.vscode/settings.local.json

# Slurm job logs (lab convention: `mkdir -p sbatch` in the submit dir)
sbatch/

# Local scratch harnesses. `_*_test.py` / `_test_*.py` are collected by pytest but are nobody's
# deliverable -- they exist to answer one question during an investigation and then stop mattering.
# Ignored so a broad `git add` cannot sweep one into a commit, which is how `_scratch2_test.py`
# reached main twice in one PR.
/tests/_*_test.py
/tests/_test_*.py

# Agent worktrees. Claude Code puts an isolated checkout here, INSIDE the repo, so every root-level
# tool walks into it: ruff lints another session's half-finished file and fails your gate, and
# `ruff format .` / `--fix` rewrites their working tree from under them. Ignoring it fixes both
# directions at once, because ruff respects gitignore. Same reason the scratch harnesses above are
# ignored: what is not ours must not be sweepable by a broad command.
/.claude/
